Philip Potter wrote:
> Ohle Claussen wrote:
> 
>> On 2007-10-30, bcr666 wrote:
>>
>>> How do I do it?  I did a n10s 3 times on the square next to the chest,
>>> I tried to #u the chest "You know of no traps here.", but when I
>>> kicked the chest, I set off a trap.
>>
>>
>> #untrap is the correct command for doing this. You have to be standing on
>> top of the chest and give "." (or possibly ">" as an alternative) as the
>> direction. "." is the standard direction command for "here". Note that a
>> single #untrap is not guaranteed to find traps on the chest.
> 
> 
> However, a single #untrap will inform you that you are doing the right 
> thing by asking "There is a chest/large box here. Check it for traps?"
> 

I've made a patch that eliminates the confirmation for untraping if 
there is only one chest or large box on location that has no other trap. 
This way you can do #untrap > ^A ^A ^A ^A to check the chest or large 
box five times.

I think it's available from Pasi's database:

http://bilious.homelinux.org/?233

It is option driven so you can have both old and new function in same 
executable.

Topi '(Un)locking and untraping shouldn't ask confirmation for a lone 
